

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next fork — this one sets the boundary of the module, i.e. how much work each new form still has to do.

  [1mThe fork:[0m does the validation module stop at pure logic, or does it also own the DOM? Today [38;5;153mapp.js[39m manually reads each
  input by id, calls [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m, and dumps failures to [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m — nothing renders to the page. Multiple forms
  means that plumbing gets repeated per form unless the module absorbs it.

  - [1mPure logic only[0m — the module exports a validator that takes a plain data object and returns results. Each form keeps
    its own code to read inputs and display errors. Smallest, trivially testable in Node with no DOM at all, but signup
    repeats login's ~10 lines of reading fields and showing messages.
  - [1mLogic + form binding[0m — the module also exports something like [38;5;153mattachValidation(formElement, schema)[39m that reads
    fields via [38;5;153mFormData[39m, runs the rules on submit, and renders messages into the page. A new form becomes a schema plus
    one call. More surface to design and test, and it makes a decision about error markup that every form inherits.
  - [1mLogic + binding + live validation[0m — the above, plus validating on blur/input rather than only on submit. Better UX,
    but it's the most behavior to specify and test, and it's easy to add later on top of the middle option.

  Cheap to change later: adding live/on-blur validation onto a binding layer. Expensive: if forms hand-roll their own
  error rendering now, unifying it later means rewriting each form's display code and its markup.

  [1mMy recommendation is logic + form binding.[0m "Reusable across multiple forms" mostly fails at the plumbing, not the
  rules — if signup still hand-writes field reading and error display, we've only shared the easy half. Keeping the
  validator itself DOM-free inside that module means the rules stay unit-testable in plain Node.
